1. PURPOSE
OneDraft shall establish the AI Evaluation, Guardrails & Deterministic Command Verification subsystem governing the transition between Aria-generated intelligence and executable changes to the OneDraft Project Model.
The purpose of this subsystem is to ensure that AI-generated architectural interpretations, commands, modifications, redline instructions and project operations are evaluated before they are permitted to modify authoritative project state.
Aria shall operate as the intelligence and generative reasoning layer.
The OneDraft verification architecture shall operate as the deterministic control layer.
Accordingly, the fundamental architecture is:
AI OUTPUT → STRUCTURED COMMAND → VERIFICATION → PROJECT MODEL
and not:
AI OUTPUT → DIRECT PROJECT MUTATION
The system must therefore distinguish between an AI-generated proposal and an authoritative project-state transition.
No AI-generated command shall directly modify the authoritative Project Model without passing through the applicable schema, authorization, project-state, constraint, geometry, compliance, coordination and execution-policy controls.

2. AI VERIFICATION IDENTITY
Every AI-generated executable operation shall receive a unique immutable Command_ID.
The command verification identity record shall contain:
Command_ID
Project_ID
Organization_ID
User_ID
Revision_ID
Model_Version
AI_Provider
AI_Model
AI_Model_Version
Prompt_Version
Context_Version
Command_Schema_Version
Guardrail_Version
Verifier_Version
Confidence_Assessment
Risk_Classification
Verification_Status
Execution_Decision
Created_Date
Verified_Date
Executed_Date
The Command_ID shall remain associated with the complete lifecycle of the AI-generated operation.
Verification shall create a traceable relationship between the original AI output, the structured command, the verification result and any resulting Project Model revision.

3. AI OUTPUT CLASSIFICATION
The system shall classify every substantive Aria output before determining whether execution is permitted.
The classification structure shall support, at minimum:
Proposal
Command
Question
Explanation
Warning
Rejection
Requires Review
A Proposal shall represent an AI-generated recommendation that has not yet been authorized for execution.
A Command shall represent a structured operation capable of being evaluated by the OneDraft verification architecture.
A Question shall identify information required before the requested operation can be reliably interpreted.
An Explanation shall contain informational output without Project Model mutation authority.
A Warning shall identify a condition requiring attention.
A Rejection shall identify an operation that cannot proceed under the applicable system controls.
A Requires Review state shall identify an operation that may be technically possible but requires human, customer or professional review before execution.
AI output classification shall therefore prevent natural-language generation from being treated as an implicit database instruction.

4. STRUCTURED COMMAND MODEL
Aria-generated executable operations shall be converted into structured OneDraft commands before they are submitted to the execution architecture.
A structured command shall identify, where applicable:
Operation
Project_ID
Revision_ID
Target_Objects
Parameters
Affected_Constraints
Required_Preservation_Rules
Reason
Confidence
Risk_Classification
Source_Reference
The structured command shall provide a deterministic representation of the intended Project Model operation.
Natural-language interpretation alone shall not constitute an executable operation.
The command schema shall therefore provide a formal boundary between Aria's generative reasoning and the transactional application services responsible for changing project state.

5. COMMAND SCHEMA VALIDATION
Every structured AI command shall undergo schema validation before further processing.
The validation process shall verify:
Required fields
Field types
Enumerated values
Identifier validity
Parameter structures
Numeric ranges
Object references
Operation compatibility
Revision identifiers
Command schema version
Malformed commands shall be rejected before they reach Project Model mutation services.
Schema validity shall not constitute authorization or architectural correctness.
A command may therefore be structurally valid while subsequently failing authorization, constraint, geometry, compliance or coordination verification.

6. AUTHORIZATION VERIFICATION
AI-generated commands shall remain subject to the Identity, Access & Security architecture established in the preceding OneDraft stacks.
The verification system shall evaluate:
User_Identity
Organization_Membership
Project_Membership
Project_Role
Command_Privilege
Resource_Ownership
Administrative_Restrictions
System_Privilege
A valid command shall not automatically be considered an authorized command.
Accordingly:
VALID COMMAND ≠ AUTHORIZED COMMAND
Authorization shall be determined through the established OneDraft access-control architecture rather than through the AI model itself.
Aria shall have no implicit authority to bypass OneDraft authorization boundaries.

7. PROJECT STATE VERIFICATION
Before execution, the command shall be evaluated against the current authoritative Project Model state.
The system shall verify:
Project exists
Project is active
Referenced revision exists
Referenced model version exists
Target objects exist
Target objects belong to the project
Target objects are available for modification
Required project state is compatible with the operation
The command shall not be permitted to modify a project state that no longer corresponds to the state against which the AI operation was generated.
Stale project context shall therefore be treated as a verification condition rather than silently reconciled against current state.

8. REVISION CONTROL
Every AI-generated command shall reference the project revision against which it was generated.
The system shall compare the command's expected revision with the current authoritative revision.
For example:
Expected Revision = 14
Current Revision = 14
The command may proceed to subsequent verification.
Where:
Expected Revision = 14
Current Revision = 15
the command shall not overwrite or silently modify Revision 15.
The system shall return a revision conflict and require the operation to be regenerated, reconciled or otherwise resolved through the applicable project workflow.
Historical project revisions shall remain immutable.

9. TARGET OBJECT VERIFICATION
Every AI-generated command affecting the Project Model shall identify the objects upon which the operation is intended to act.
The verification system shall determine whether:
The target object exists
The target object belongs to the project
The target object supports the requested operation
The target object is editable
The target object is not deleted
The target object is not superseded
The target object is not locked against the operation
The target object maintains valid relationships with the Project Model
An operation shall not be permitted to act upon an object that does not satisfy the applicable target conditions.

10. PARAMETER VERIFICATION
AI-generated parameters shall be independently validated before they are applied to the Project Model.
Parameter verification shall support architectural values including:
Dimensions
Coordinates
Elevations
Angles
Offsets
Clearances
Wall Thicknesses
Door Widths
Room Areas
Slopes
Heights
Grid Positions
Spacing
Rotation
Scale
The AI model may propose parameter values.
The deterministic OneDraft systems shall determine whether those values are valid within the current Project Model, applicable constraints and configured project rules.
AI confidence shall not substitute for parameter validation.

11. CONSTRAINT PRESERVATION
Constraint preservation shall constitute a primary verification function of the subsystem.
The system shall identify constraints affected by an AI-generated operation.
Constraints may include:
Property Boundaries
Setbacks
Structural Grids
Structural Members
Fire Separations
Egress Paths
Accessible Routes
Minimum Clearances
Floor-to-Floor Heights
Shaft Locations
Mechanical Zones
Plumbing Stacks
Existing Conditions
Survey Coordinates
Client-Locked Dimensions
Jurisdictional Restrictions
Approved Redlines
The system shall determine whether execution of the proposed command would preserve, modify, violate, delete or ambiguously affect the applicable constraints.

12. CONSTRAINT DECISION
The constraint verification system shall support, at minimum:
Preserved
Modified with Authorization
Violated
Ambiguous
A preserved constraint shall permit continued verification.
A modified constraint shall require the applicable authorization and state transition.
A violated constraint shall cause the command to be rejected unless the governing workflow explicitly permits the constraint itself to be revised.
An ambiguous constraint shall cause the command to require review or additional information.
Ambiguity shall never be interpreted as authorization.

13. GEOMETRIC VERIFICATION
Any command affecting architectural geometry shall be evaluated through the computational geometry architecture established in the preceding OneDraft stacks.
Geometric verification shall support:
Topological Validity
Intersection Detection
Collision Detection
Clearance
Connectivity
Containment
Alignment
Dimension Consistency
Closed-Loop Geometry
Room Boundary Integrity
Wall Connectivity
Opening Containment
Level Association
Coordinate Consistency
The AI model shall not constitute the authoritative source of geometric correctness.
The OneDraft geometry engine shall remain authoritative for deterministic geometric computation.

14. REGULATORY VERIFICATION
Where an AI-generated command affects a regulated project condition, the command shall be evaluated through the Compliance & Regulatory Engine and Validation & Verification Engine established in the preceding architecture.
The regulatory verification sequence shall remain:
CODE MANIFEST → CODE RULES → APPLICABILITY → PROJECT MODEL → COMMAND EFFECT → VALIDATION
The AI model shall not independently create or override the authoritative regulatory baseline.
The applicable jurisdictional code manifest shall remain associated with the project state against which the command is evaluated.

15. COORDINATION VERIFICATION
AI-generated commands shall be evaluated for effects upon coordinated project systems where applicable.
The coordination architecture shall support:
Architectural
Structural
Mechanical
Electrical
Plumbing
Life Safety
Accessibility
Site
Civil
Vertical Circulation
Fire Protection
A modification to one project element may affect multiple dependent elements.
The system shall therefore evaluate downstream effects where such dependencies are represented within the Project Model.
An unresolved coordination conflict shall prevent the command from being represented as a fully verified project-state transition.

16. AI CONFIDENCE
Aria may provide a confidence assessment associated with a generated interpretation or command.
Confidence shall be treated as an AI assessment rather than a deterministic proof of correctness.
A confidence value shall not represent:
Legal correctness
Structural correctness
Regulatory approval
Municipal approval
Professional certification
Building-permit approval
Confidence shall instead represent the AI system's assessment of its own generated interpretation under the applicable model and context.
Deterministic OneDraft verification shall remain authoritative.

17. CONFIDENCE POLICY
The system shall support configurable confidence thresholds.
A high-confidence command may proceed toward execution where all mandatory deterministic checks pass.
A medium-confidence command may require additional verification or review.
A low-confidence command may be rejected or routed for review.
The system shall enforce the following principle:
HIGH AI CONFIDENCE + DETERMINISTIC FAILURE = REJECT
AI confidence shall never override:
Constraint failure
Geometry failure
Compliance failure
Authorization failure
Revision conflict
Coordination failure
Schema failure

18. GUARDRAIL ARCHITECTURE
The OneDraft guardrail architecture shall operate across multiple control boundaries.
The system shall support:
Input Guardrails
Context Guardrails
Output Guardrails
Command Guardrails
Execution Guardrails
Compliance Guardrails
Audit Guardrails
Input guardrails shall identify incomplete, conflicting or ambiguous project instructions.
Context guardrails shall ensure that Aria receives project information corresponding to the applicable project state.
Output guardrails shall evaluate generated responses before they are transformed into executable operations.
Command guardrails shall control structured commands immediately before deterministic execution.
Execution guardrails shall determine whether the verified command is eligible for autonomous execution.
Audit guardrails shall ensure that the resulting operation remains traceable.

19. INPUT GUARDRAILS
Input guardrails shall identify conditions that may prevent reliable interpretation of the customer's instructions.
The system shall identify:
Missing Dimensions
Conflicting Specifications
Ambiguous Object References
Missing Project State
Unsupported Operations
Incomplete Requirements
Conflicting Constraints
Unresolved Redlines
The system shall distinguish between missing information and negative information.
Missing information shall not be interpreted as permission to invent project requirements.
Where necessary, Aria shall request additional project information before generating an executable command.

20. CONTEXT GUARDRAILS
The context supplied to Aria shall be bounded by the authoritative project information available to the current command.
Context may include:
Current Project Revision
Current Model Version
Project Requirements
Project Constraints
Project Geometry
Code Manifest
Validation Results
Active Redlines
Approved Changes
Locked Objects
Customer Instructions
Stale project information shall not be silently represented as current project state.
Context construction shall remain coordinated with the AI Intelligence & Model Provider Architecture established in the preceding stack.

21. OUTPUT GUARDRAILS
Aria output shall be evaluated before it is accepted as a structured command.
The system shall identify:
Malformed Output
Unsupported Operations
Missing Parameters
Contradictory Instructions
Unsupported Assumptions
Unresolved Ambiguity
Invalid Object References
Unsafe State Transitions
Output that cannot be deterministically interpreted shall not proceed to Project Model execution.

22. COMMAND GUARDRAILS
Command guardrails shall operate immediately before Project Model mutation.
The command guardrail layer shall verify:
Command Identity
Authorization
Project Revision
Target Objects
Parameters
Constraints
Dependencies
Operation Policy
Execution Policy
The command shall not proceed to transactional mutation until the required verification conditions have been satisfied.

23. EXECUTION DECISION
Every AI-generated command shall receive an explicit execution decision.
The decision model shall support:
Execute
Reject
Require Review
Retry
Regenerate
Defer
Execute shall indicate that all mandatory verification conditions have passed.
Reject shall indicate that the command cannot proceed.
Require Review shall indicate that the operation requires an additional decision.
Retry shall indicate a recoverable processing condition.
Regenerate shall indicate that the AI output itself is insufficient for reliable execution.
Defer shall indicate that execution depends upon another project state transition.

24. DETERMINISTIC COMMAND VERIFICATION
The command verification system shall evaluate every executable AI command against deterministic project information and versioned verification rules.
The verification function shall conceptually evaluate:
Project State
Command
Authorization
Constraints
Code Manifest
Validation Rules
Coordination State
Execution Policy
The verification result shall provide a deterministic decision for a fixed set of inputs, rules and verifier versions.
The verification engine shall therefore create a formal boundary between probabilistic AI generation and deterministic project execution.

