The verification record shall not be modified merely because a later project revision supersedes the command.

30. DETERMINISTIC REPLAY
The system shall support deterministic replay of verification decisions where the required historical inputs remain available.
Replay shall use the applicable:
Project Revision
Project Model Version
Command
Constraints
Code Manifest
Verifier Version
Command Schema
Guardrail Version
The purpose of replay is to establish whether the same deterministic verification architecture produces the same result from the same authoritative inputs.

31. AI AND DETERMINISTIC SYSTEM BOUNDARY
The OneDraft architecture shall explicitly distinguish between the probabilistic AI layer and the deterministic project-control layer.
The AI layer shall provide:
Interpretation
Generation
Reasoning
Proposal
Command Construction
The deterministic layer shall provide:
Schema Validation
Authorization
State Validation
Constraint Verification
Geometry Verification
Compliance Verification
Coordination Verification
Execution Policy
Transactional Mutation
Audit
The system shall not attempt to make the AI model itself the authoritative deterministic system.
Instead, OneDraft shall make the transition from AI output to authoritative Project Model state controlled and verifiable.

32. HUMAN REVIEW ESCALATION
The system shall support escalation where an AI-generated operation cannot safely proceed under autonomous execution policy.
Escalation conditions may include:
Insufficient Confidence
Ambiguous Requirements
Multiple Valid Interpretations
Constraint Conflicts
Unresolved Coordination
Locked Project Conditions
High-Risk Operations
Required Professional Review
The resulting review decision shall become part of the project revision history where it affects Project Model state.

33. CUSTOMER REVIEW
Customer review shall remain a defined stage within the OneDraft project lifecycle.
Customers shall be able to:
Review
Redline
Modify Instructions
Reject Proposals
Request Regeneration
Approve Permitted Operations
Customer approval shall remain distinguishable from automated deterministic verification.
The system shall preserve the relationship between customer instruction, AI interpretation, system verification and resulting project state.

34. REDLINE VERIFICATION
Redlines processed through Aria shall enter the same command-verification architecture as other AI-generated operations.
The redline workflow shall therefore be:
REDLINE → ARIA INTERPRETATION → STRUCTURED COMMAND → VERIFICATION → PROJECT MODEL → DOCUMENTATION
A redline shall not automatically constitute permission for an unverified Project Model mutation.
The resulting revision shall remain traceable to the originating redline.

35. AUTONOMOUS EXECUTION
OneDraft may permit autonomous execution for operations falling within a configured execution policy.
Operations may include, where permitted:
Dimension Adjustments
Annotation Placement
Sheet Composition
Graphic Corrections
Alignment Operations
Standardized Drafting Refinements
User-Authorized Spatial Adjustments
Execution policy shall be configurable according to project state, command type, customer settings and applicable review requirements.
High-impact operations may require explicit review before execution.

36. COMMAND RISK CLASSIFICATION
AI-generated commands shall support risk classification.
The minimum classification structure shall support:
Class A — Low Impact
Class B — Moderate Impact
Class C — High Impact
Class D — Review Required
Risk classification may consider:
Affected Object Type
Number of Affected Objects
Structural Relevance
Life-Safety Relevance
Code Relevance
Geometry Complexity
Coordination Dependencies
Project Stage
Customer Policy
Professional Review Requirements
Risk classification shall supplement, and shall not replace, deterministic validation.

37. CASCADE IMPACT ANALYSIS
The verification system shall identify downstream project effects where the Project Model contains the required relationships.
For example:
WALL MODIFICATION → ROOM BOUNDARY → DOOR LOCATION → EGRESS → ACCESSIBILITY → MECHANICAL ROUTING → DOCUMENTATION
A command shall therefore be evaluated not only against its direct target objects but also against affected dependent objects where such relationships are represented in the Project Model.

38. SAFE PROJECT STATE TRANSITION
An AI-generated command shall be accepted only where the resulting Project Model state can be established as a valid state transition.
The fundamental transaction shall be:
CURRENT PROJECT STATE + VALIDATED COMMAND → VALID NEXT PROJECT STATE
Where the proposed state cannot satisfy the required verification conditions:
CURRENT PROJECT STATE + INVALID COMMAND → REJECT
The authoritative current project state shall remain unchanged following a rejected operation.

39. TRANSACTIONAL EXECUTION
Verification and Project Model mutation shall remain logically distinct while being coordinated through the transactional application architecture.
The intended execution sequence shall be:
VERIFY → BEGIN TRANSACTION → RECHECK REVISION → APPLY MUTATION → CREATE MODEL VERSION → CREATE REVISION → CREATE CHANGE RECORDS → UPDATE CURRENT STATE → WRITE AUDIT EVENT → COMMIT
Where execution fails before commit, the transaction shall be rolled back.
The system shall not permit a partially committed AI-generated architectural operation to become authoritative Project Model state.

40. IDEMPOTENCY AND RETRY SAFETY
AI commands shall support idempotent execution where applicable.
The system shall support:
Command_ID
Request_ID
Idempotency_Key
Repeated requests shall not unintentionally produce duplicate Project Model mutations.
Retry behavior shall distinguish between an unsuccessful AI generation request and a Project Model transaction whose outcome has already been committed.
A committed operation shall not be repeated without first determining its authoritative transaction state.

